#Gutenberg Corpus

Shared utilities for computing statistics over Project Gutenberg
plaintext editions.  Each edition follows a common format: a prose
file with a preamble and postscript bounded by sentinel lines.  The
functions here are independent of any particular text; they apply
uniformly to any Gutenberg play or novel.

A second text — Titus Andronicus, say, or The Great Gatsby — would
be a module of the same shape as ##Gutenberg Hamlet, importing this
one for the analysis machinery.

##Loading

A Gutenberg file is bounded by sentinel lines of the form
`*** START OF THE PROJECT GUTENBERG EBOOK ...` and
`*** END OF THE PROJECT GUTENBERG EBOOK ...`.  Slicing between
them strips the legal preamble and postscript, leaving the plain
text of the work.

    def load_play(path: Path) -> str:
        """Strip Gutenberg header and footer; return play text."""
        raw   = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8")
        start = raw.index("*** START OF THE PROJECT GUTENBERG")
        end   = raw.index("*** END OF THE PROJECT GUTENBERG")
        return raw[raw.index("\n", start) + 1 : end]

##Word Frequencies

Stripping punctuation and lowercasing gives a flat word list; a
Counter over that list is the frequency distribution.

    def word_frequencies(text: str) -> Counter[str]:
        words = re.findall(r"[a-z]+", text.lower())
        return Counter(words)

~example
    word_frequencies("") == Counter()
    word_frequencies("the the cat") == Counter({"the": 2, "cat": 1})
    word_frequencies("THE CAT") == Counter({"the": 1, "cat": 1})

The total word count equals the number of words found by the same
regex.  This holds for any input — it is a property of the function
itself, not a fact about any particular text.

~property
    @given(text=st.text(max_size=200))
    def _(text):
        freqs = word_frequencies(text)
        assert sum(freqs.values()) == len(
            re.findall(r"[a-z]+", text.lower())
        )

##Speaking Parts

In a Gutenberg play, each speech is introduced by the speaker's name
in ALL CAPS followed by a period on its own line.  Counting these
labels gives turn counts by character.  Counting turns rather than
lines avoids inflating characters who speak in long monologues.

    def parse_speakers(text: str) -> Counter[str]:
        turns: Counter[str] = Counter()
        for line in text.splitlines():
            m = re.match(r'^([A-Z][A-Z ]+)\.$', line.strip())
            if m:
                turns[m.group(1)] += 1
        return turns

~example
    parse_speakers("") == Counter()
    parse_speakers("HAMLET.\nTo be or not to be.") == Counter({"HAMLET": 1})
    (parse_speakers("HAMLET.\nHORATIO.\n")
     == Counter({"HAMLET": 1, "HORATIO": 1}))

---

#Tests

##word frequencies
    word_frequencies("hello world") == Counter({"hello": 1, "world": 1})
    word_frequencies("THE THE") == Counter({"the": 2})
    word_frequencies("123 !!! ...") == Counter()
    sum(word_frequencies("one two three").values()) == 3

##speaking parts
    (parse_speakers("HAMLET.\nTo be or not to be.")
     == Counter({"HAMLET": 1}))
    (parse_speakers("KING CLAUDIUS.\nHAMLET.\n")
     == Counter({"KING CLAUDIUS": 1, "HAMLET": 1}))
    parse_speakers("hamlet.\n") == Counter()
    parse_speakers("Not a speaker line") == Counter()

#References
    from pathlib import Path
    from collections import Counter
    import re
